WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:07.160
MIKE XU: Hi, everyone.

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:08.830
Welcome to our talk today.

00:00:08.830 --> 00:00:12.310
We're going to be talking about
building data applications,

00:00:12.310 --> 00:00:15.120
powered by Looker.

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:16.770
My is Mike Xu--

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:18.390
I'm an architect at Google.

00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:21.690
I help our customers
design, architect and build

00:00:21.690 --> 00:00:25.080
their applications using
the Looker platform.

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:28.417
And today, I'm also joined by
my friend and colleague, Nick.

00:00:28.417 --> 00:00:29.500
NICK FOGLER: Hey everyone.

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:30.780
I'm Nick Fogler.

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:33.900
The founder and CEO
of 4 Mile Analytics.

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:36.780
And we're a strategic
Looker partner,

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:39.840
really specializing in the kind
of customer data experiences

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:41.727
built on top of
the Looker platform

00:00:41.727 --> 00:00:43.310
features we'll be
talking about today.

00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:49.640
MIKE XU: So for our
agenda in our talk today,

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:52.550
we're going to be covering
Looker as a platform--

00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:56.000
all the different facets
of Looker's platform

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:57.092
to build your application.

00:00:57.092 --> 00:00:58.550
And also, we're
going to be looking

00:00:58.550 --> 00:01:02.060
at a case study of building
an application using one

00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:03.860
of these facets,
which is the API,

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:06.020
for a global Fortune
100 retailer.

00:01:09.230 --> 00:01:12.590
So Looker originally was built
as a business intelligence

00:01:12.590 --> 00:01:18.830
and analytics tool for analysts,
marketers and product managers.

00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:22.370
It has this really awesome
layer of technology

00:01:22.370 --> 00:01:26.840
called Look ML, the Look
Modeling Language, which allows

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:29.580
you to express business logic.

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:33.110
And then your end users
can derive insights

00:01:33.110 --> 00:01:36.380
from the data with
no code at all

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:38.270
so they don't need to
know SQL and they don't

00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:41.340
need to run MAP-produced jobs.

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:44.300
So it's really evolved, now, to
become an extensible platform

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:46.580
to power other
applications so you

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:49.340
can get the richness of
every data source connected

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:53.190
to Looker that has Look
ML built on top of it.

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:57.215
You can also leverage Looker's
governance and security model,

00:01:57.215 --> 00:01:59.090
as well as borrow from
its front end as well.

00:02:02.755 --> 00:02:04.380
So we're not really
going to be talking

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:05.790
too much about Looker today--

00:02:05.790 --> 00:02:07.890
there's plenty of content
out there available.

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:11.430
We're going to be primarily
focused on Looker's platform.

00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:15.360
So there's three different
facets that Looker offers.

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:17.250
The embed and the API--

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:21.960
these are ways of getting
Looker into your application.

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:23.790
And the extension
framework is really

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:27.540
to change Looker so that way
it becomes more the application

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:29.160
that you want.

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:30.480
The Embedded Looker.

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:35.090
This is the way of getting
visual elements within Looker,

00:02:35.090 --> 00:02:38.510
such as dashboards, or
explorers, or saved reports,

00:02:38.510 --> 00:02:41.560
and you pluck that right
into your application.

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:43.650
If you don't like the way
that Lookers dashboards

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:47.640
or reports look, that's what
the API and SDK are for.

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:49.320
These are lower-level
building blocks

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:52.560
that we offer so you can build
a completely bespoke experience

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:53.702
from scratch.

00:02:53.702 --> 00:02:55.410
And for a case study,
Nick will primarily

00:02:55.410 --> 00:02:59.660
be focused on
looking at the API.

00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:01.450
Finally, we have the
extension framework.

00:03:01.450 --> 00:03:03.760
The extension
framework is, again,

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:06.400
to mold Looker to be
whatever you want,

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:08.330
whatever suits your data needs.

00:03:08.330 --> 00:03:10.960
You can use your own
custom JavaScript directly

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:11.650
within Looker.

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:18.110
So let's dive into the first
facet of Looker's platform--

00:03:18.110 --> 00:03:19.820
the Embedded Looker.

00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:22.530
So here we have some
application code.

00:03:22.530 --> 00:03:24.450
This is on your
server application.

00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:27.150
It's actually one
of Looker's SDK's.

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:29.450
This is our JavaScript SDK.

00:03:29.450 --> 00:03:31.970
We provide our SDK in
many different languages,

00:03:31.970 --> 00:03:35.450
such as Java, Python, Go--

00:03:35.450 --> 00:03:37.870
whatever you would like to use.

00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:40.440
So the first thing we
need to do on our server

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:43.650
is to provide
which Looker server

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:47.100
we're going to request
the embed from, and also

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:50.040
the shared secret
to authenticate

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:52.670
our application to that Looker.

00:03:52.670 --> 00:03:55.560
Once we've taken care of
the security configurations,

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:59.130
we can now define some
user context for our embed.

00:03:59.130 --> 00:04:02.800
So the user context really
serves two different purposes.

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:05.400
First, we get end-to-end
tracing of that user

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:09.210
as they're using our native
application and Looker.

00:04:09.210 --> 00:04:12.150
And secondly, we're also
tightly coupling the security

00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:15.150
between the native
application and Looker.

00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:17.380
We identified the user--

00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:20.339
we can either use a single
sign-on service available

00:04:20.339 --> 00:04:22.019
if we already have
that built out,

00:04:22.019 --> 00:04:24.840
or we can just use the
native applications metadata

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:28.040
and provide some sort of
user ID to the Looker.

00:04:28.040 --> 00:04:31.130
This context is also really
important to provide to Looker

00:04:31.130 --> 00:04:32.990
because this is
what governs what

00:04:32.990 --> 00:04:35.120
they can see in Looker
in terms of data

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:36.740
and also which
features in Looker

00:04:36.740 --> 00:04:41.035
they have access to
within that embed.

00:04:41.035 --> 00:04:42.910
Now we're going to switch
gears a little bit.

00:04:42.910 --> 00:04:45.580
We're going to jump into the
front end of our application.

00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:48.220
We're finally going to
instantiate our embed.

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:50.860
Once the embed is
instantiated, we

00:04:50.860 --> 00:04:53.770
can couple that with
the native application

00:04:53.770 --> 00:04:55.600
through the event bus.

00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:59.140
In this example, we're modifying
which buttons are actually

00:04:59.140 --> 00:05:01.750
driving the
dashboard's interaction

00:05:01.750 --> 00:05:05.530
by binding listeners in our
native application on top

00:05:05.530 --> 00:05:08.830
of Looker's embed.

00:05:08.830 --> 00:05:11.050
So here's what it looks like.

00:05:11.050 --> 00:05:14.320
We have our native application
outside of this red outline.

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:17.550
And within the red outline,
we have Embedded Looker.

00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:20.560
You can see, if you're familiar
with Looker's dashboards,

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:22.980
this is just the themed
Looker dashboard.

00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:26.040
But if you notice,
there's no filters--

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:28.170
like, you don't see
Looker's native filters.

00:05:28.170 --> 00:05:29.820
We're actually
building the filters

00:05:29.820 --> 00:05:33.060
outside within the native
application and coupling that

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:38.850
to the Looker embedded
dashboard through the event bus.

00:05:38.850 --> 00:05:40.860
You can imagine how
this can rapidly

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:42.773
accelerate your
development of application

00:05:42.773 --> 00:05:44.190
because you're not
really building

00:05:44.190 --> 00:05:46.530
too much front-end code or
data visualization code.

00:05:48.842 --> 00:05:50.300
So if you want
something different,

00:05:50.300 --> 00:05:52.960
though-- like if our
dashboards or reports really

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:54.940
don't meet your
needs, that's really

00:05:54.940 --> 00:05:57.070
when we start
recommending the API.

00:05:57.070 --> 00:06:00.580
So here's our API
documentation page.

00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:04.210
And you can see the large list
of different methods we offer.

00:06:04.210 --> 00:06:06.670
These actually are the
methods that we use internally

00:06:06.670 --> 00:06:11.650
to build Looker itself,
and we offer them to you

00:06:11.650 --> 00:06:13.580
to build your applications.

00:06:13.580 --> 00:06:17.578
So you have 100% coverage of
what Looker can do directly

00:06:17.578 --> 00:06:18.245
through our API.

00:06:21.830 --> 00:06:23.920
Here's an example of
an API application

00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:27.130
that Nick has built
with his team at 4 Mile.

00:06:27.130 --> 00:06:30.310
So again, if you're familiar
with Looker, right off the bat,

00:06:30.310 --> 00:06:33.880
this looks like a native
explorer in Looker.

00:06:33.880 --> 00:06:35.380
But this is actually
very different.

00:06:35.380 --> 00:06:37.090
In this [? second ?]
application,

00:06:37.090 --> 00:06:39.940
we're really building the
experience for the user

00:06:39.940 --> 00:06:42.310
to be working with
different audience segments

00:06:42.310 --> 00:06:44.500
and cohorts in mind.

00:06:44.500 --> 00:06:46.480
So you're really just
comparing cohorts,

00:06:46.480 --> 00:06:48.970
and looking at the
results between them,

00:06:48.970 --> 00:06:53.310
instead of building these
complex filter groups.

00:06:53.310 --> 00:06:55.290
Here we're building
out one of the cohorts

00:06:55.290 --> 00:06:59.080
using the attributes available
within our user data set.

00:06:59.080 --> 00:07:01.862
And once we've built out this
cohort, or multiple cohorts,

00:07:01.862 --> 00:07:03.570
we can start doing
reporting against them

00:07:03.570 --> 00:07:08.850
to search for outliers, or
maybe positive or negative

00:07:08.850 --> 00:07:12.660
performances, within a
specific area of that cohort.

00:07:12.660 --> 00:07:15.510
So, again, if you're familiar
with Looker's Query Builder,

00:07:15.510 --> 00:07:17.760
this is a much lighter
weight and much more

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:21.570
custom-tailored experience
designed for marketers who

00:07:21.570 --> 00:07:22.965
are focused on cohort research.

00:07:26.920 --> 00:07:29.050
Here we have another
application that

00:07:29.050 --> 00:07:30.490
was built on top of our API--

00:07:30.490 --> 00:07:33.490
this was built for
global payments.

00:07:33.490 --> 00:07:35.620
And this was a
merchant portal that

00:07:35.620 --> 00:07:38.570
allows the merchants to see
their transactions over time.

00:07:38.570 --> 00:07:42.130
So they can see each of their
given customers, what they're

00:07:42.130 --> 00:07:43.985
making transactions
on, when they made it

00:07:43.985 --> 00:07:45.610
and what kind of
metadata is associated

00:07:45.610 --> 00:07:47.510
with those transactions.

00:07:47.510 --> 00:07:51.370
We also have top-level reporting
to see, overall, the cash flow

00:07:51.370 --> 00:07:54.300
throughout the day.

00:07:54.300 --> 00:07:56.480
We're also going to be
diving more into the API

00:07:56.480 --> 00:07:57.710
when we cover our case study.

00:08:00.370 --> 00:08:05.020
Lastly, this is the newest facet
to Looker's platform, which

00:08:05.020 --> 00:08:06.940
is the Extension framework.

00:08:06.940 --> 00:08:10.580
So the Extension framework is
very different from the Embed

00:08:10.580 --> 00:08:12.070
and API route.

00:08:12.070 --> 00:08:14.650
Because you really don't
have to host anything--

00:08:14.650 --> 00:08:18.160
you don't have any
application server yourself.

00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:20.520
You don't have a
back-end or a front-end.

00:08:20.520 --> 00:08:22.370
Looker is all of that.

00:08:22.370 --> 00:08:26.920
So what you're doing is sending
us your custom JavaScript,

00:08:26.920 --> 00:08:28.570
which will be encapsulated.

00:08:28.570 --> 00:08:30.820
So that way your data
sources are still protected,

00:08:30.820 --> 00:08:32.690
and also you're
Looker is protected.

00:08:32.690 --> 00:08:35.080
But you're still able to
get the data experience

00:08:35.080 --> 00:08:38.059
that you really want.

00:08:38.059 --> 00:08:44.179
So here, this extension
was built by 4 Mile--

00:08:44.179 --> 00:08:45.440
Nick's team, again.

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:47.980
If you notice, we are
still inside Looker--

00:08:47.980 --> 00:08:50.330
we have the Looker
menu bar at the top.

00:08:50.330 --> 00:08:52.940
But this is a highly
branded experience.

00:08:52.940 --> 00:08:56.560
We've augmented Looker with the
user's native context in mind

00:08:56.560 --> 00:08:59.350
to help them discover insights
and, hopefully, increase

00:08:59.350 --> 00:09:00.910
action ability.

00:09:00.910 --> 00:09:06.070
We're making Looker feel
more like home to them.

00:09:06.070 --> 00:09:10.180
In this next dashboard, we're
overlaying multiple metrics.

00:09:10.180 --> 00:09:14.230
So for this example, this
is a sportswear company,

00:09:14.230 --> 00:09:18.580
and we're looking at how
weather is impacting the supply

00:09:18.580 --> 00:09:23.380
chain in terms of orders and
dollar value to the business.

00:09:23.380 --> 00:09:26.280
So, hopefully, you can see how
the Extension framework really

00:09:26.280 --> 00:09:29.070
helps you build these
experiences that just having

00:09:29.070 --> 00:09:31.620
tables and graphs
don't necessarily help

00:09:31.620 --> 00:09:33.390
the users in drawing insights.

00:09:33.390 --> 00:09:34.890
If you want something
really, really

00:09:34.890 --> 00:09:38.010
custom or unique in
Looker, we encourage

00:09:38.010 --> 00:09:39.528
using the Extension framework.

00:09:42.160 --> 00:09:44.180
So if you're interested
in learning more about,

00:09:44.180 --> 00:09:48.440
also, how other people are using
Looker as a platform, or more

00:09:48.440 --> 00:09:51.410
about how other people are
using each of these facets,

00:09:51.410 --> 00:09:53.990
there's lots of different
resources available.

00:09:53.990 --> 00:09:56.710
First, there is a
Looker Blocks directory.

00:09:56.710 --> 00:09:59.400
These are just code
examples, snippets,

00:09:59.400 --> 00:10:04.050
that you can take and drop
right in your application.

00:10:04.050 --> 00:10:07.220
We also have community
libraries, community tools,

00:10:07.220 --> 00:10:10.580
such as linters,
stress testing tools.

00:10:10.580 --> 00:10:12.480
And, finally, there's
LookML patterns.

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:14.780
So if you have a specific
type of data set,

00:10:14.780 --> 00:10:18.110
we already have the best ways
of analyzing that data set--

00:10:18.110 --> 00:10:19.970
and you can just
steal that from us.

00:10:22.720 --> 00:10:25.090
So with that, I'm
going to turn it over

00:10:25.090 --> 00:10:28.060
to Nick so that we can look at a
case study of actually building

00:10:28.060 --> 00:10:31.270
an application on top
of the Looker platform.

00:10:31.270 --> 00:10:33.450
NICK FOGLER: Thank
you so much, Mike.

00:10:33.450 --> 00:10:35.850
So we at 4 Mile had
the great pleasure

00:10:35.850 --> 00:10:39.180
of partnering with Mike
and his team over at Looker

00:10:39.180 --> 00:10:41.160
recently on a
yearlong engagement

00:10:41.160 --> 00:10:44.580
to build a large-scale
data application on top

00:10:44.580 --> 00:10:47.950
of the Looker API for a
major global retailer.

00:10:47.950 --> 00:10:49.620
And I'm super excited
to be here today

00:10:49.620 --> 00:10:52.860
with you to share a little bit
about the underlying business

00:10:52.860 --> 00:10:54.690
need that we are
trying to address,

00:10:54.690 --> 00:10:58.390
the technical approach we
took and some of the outcomes.

00:10:58.390 --> 00:11:00.870
So let's dive in.

00:11:00.870 --> 00:11:03.000
Our starting point
on this engagement

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:05.640
was an internal
analytics application

00:11:05.640 --> 00:11:08.550
that our customer
had previously built

00:11:08.550 --> 00:11:11.850
whose goal is really
to serve metrics

00:11:11.850 --> 00:11:14.110
to their retail merchants.

00:11:14.110 --> 00:11:16.560
So the merchants
are the folks that

00:11:16.560 --> 00:11:18.720
make key decisions
about what to stock

00:11:18.720 --> 00:11:21.570
on the shelves, from
which vendors to purchase,

00:11:21.570 --> 00:11:23.350
and how to price each item.

00:11:23.350 --> 00:11:28.680
So really kind of foundational
stuff for any retail business.

00:11:28.680 --> 00:11:31.230
At the scale that this
particular customer of ours

00:11:31.230 --> 00:11:34.740
operates, pricing decisions
involving pennies either way

00:11:34.740 --> 00:11:38.310
can have outcomes implications
in the millions of dollars.

00:11:38.310 --> 00:11:42.823
So all of this decision-making
support is very strategic.

00:11:42.823 --> 00:11:44.490
This application had
been in development

00:11:44.490 --> 00:11:46.200
for about two years.

00:11:46.200 --> 00:11:49.380
They had had about 100
people plus working on it.

00:11:49.380 --> 00:11:51.180
And, generally,
the consensus was

00:11:51.180 --> 00:11:54.180
that there was insufficient
level of engagement.

00:11:54.180 --> 00:11:57.540
People just really weren't
adopting the application,

00:11:57.540 --> 00:12:01.170
and the return on investment
just wasn't there.

00:12:01.170 --> 00:12:05.910
Additionally, I think because of
the lack of speed and agility,

00:12:05.910 --> 00:12:08.640
they were really focusing on a
one-size-fits-all all approach

00:12:08.640 --> 00:12:10.170
to their analytics--

00:12:10.170 --> 00:12:12.413
so it was very top down.

00:12:12.413 --> 00:12:14.580
The bottom line was that
the application just really

00:12:14.580 --> 00:12:16.680
wasn't keeping pace
with the analytic needs

00:12:16.680 --> 00:12:18.270
of this organization.

00:12:18.270 --> 00:12:21.960
And without access to timely
and relevant insights,

00:12:21.960 --> 00:12:24.510
merchants were just
not really using data

00:12:24.510 --> 00:12:27.550
to drive their decision-making.

00:12:27.550 --> 00:12:31.290
So our mandate, if you
will, is to come in and just

00:12:31.290 --> 00:12:35.220
highly accelerate the
delivery of the right insights

00:12:35.220 --> 00:12:37.620
to the right people
at the right time--

00:12:37.620 --> 00:12:40.530
and ultimately, to help
drive the fundamentals

00:12:40.530 --> 00:12:42.050
of this business through data.

00:12:45.690 --> 00:12:48.570
Broadly speaking, I
think any BI application

00:12:48.570 --> 00:12:51.060
will be composed of a
couple of different layers.

00:12:51.060 --> 00:12:53.700
On one hand, you'll have
a data back-end end,

00:12:53.700 --> 00:12:56.470
typically consisting of
some kind of storage--

00:12:56.470 --> 00:12:58.860
and a query engine
sitting on top of that.

00:12:58.860 --> 00:13:02.220
And then you'll have
application code, which

00:13:02.220 --> 00:13:04.650
serves to provide
a user interface,

00:13:04.650 --> 00:13:09.360
and allow data exploration
and data visualizations.

00:13:09.360 --> 00:13:11.730
We really saw opportunities
in both of these layers

00:13:11.730 --> 00:13:14.820
to drive further acceleration.

00:13:14.820 --> 00:13:18.150
On the data back-end
side, our customer

00:13:18.150 --> 00:13:20.070
was using Thought
Spot, along with a very

00:13:20.070 --> 00:13:24.230
manual and time-consuming
data engineering workflow.

00:13:24.230 --> 00:13:27.810
And as a result when a
product person wanted

00:13:27.810 --> 00:13:30.120
to publish a new insight,
it would take weeks,

00:13:30.120 --> 00:13:33.180
and sometimes months, to
get that in front of users

00:13:33.180 --> 00:13:36.150
to even find out if it had
any kind of business value.

00:13:36.150 --> 00:13:40.290
So iteration cycles were
just taking way too long.

00:13:40.290 --> 00:13:44.820
Looker talks about this
problem as the data bread lines

00:13:44.820 --> 00:13:48.000
problem, in which you stand
in a queue for a long time

00:13:48.000 --> 00:13:51.030
and you wait to get your
little nugget of wisdom.

00:13:51.030 --> 00:13:53.920
And then having gotten
a little taste for it,

00:13:53.920 --> 00:13:56.100
you now have to get back
in the back of the queue

00:13:56.100 --> 00:13:59.170
and wait all over again.

00:13:59.170 --> 00:14:02.050
The real wonder of data
analytics, in my opinion,

00:14:02.050 --> 00:14:05.350
is that good answers
beget new questions.

00:14:05.350 --> 00:14:07.750
And if your goal
really is to build

00:14:07.750 --> 00:14:10.660
a data-driven
organization and culture,

00:14:10.660 --> 00:14:12.970
your data stack needs
to be responsive to

00:14:12.970 --> 00:14:16.740
this virtuous cycle of inquiry.

00:14:16.740 --> 00:14:19.810
Looker is really built to
solve this exact problem--

00:14:19.810 --> 00:14:22.430
it's kind of its core
value proposition.

00:14:22.430 --> 00:14:25.150
And by adding Looker
to the stack, now

00:14:25.150 --> 00:14:27.480
new questions can
be answered without

00:14:27.480 --> 00:14:31.570
this big, long roundtrip of
moving and transforming data,

00:14:31.570 --> 00:14:33.900
but rather with just simple
changes to Looker's data

00:14:33.900 --> 00:14:37.720
model via LookML, which
Mike referred to earlier.

00:14:37.720 --> 00:14:40.620
And those changes can be
done practically in real time

00:14:40.620 --> 00:14:44.880
and by people with much less
technical sophistication.

00:14:44.880 --> 00:14:47.890
So just by applying
Looker to the stack,

00:14:47.890 --> 00:14:51.810
we brought a huge increase
in speed and flexibility.

00:14:51.810 --> 00:14:55.530
On the application code
side, we replaced a lot

00:14:55.530 --> 00:14:59.010
of verbose and poorly
encapsulated code

00:14:59.010 --> 00:15:02.790
with an application development
toolkit that we built,

00:15:02.790 --> 00:15:05.820
which gives engineers the
means to deliver new data

00:15:05.820 --> 00:15:07.680
insights just an
order of magnitude

00:15:07.680 --> 00:15:10.740
more quickly and with
a lot less complexity.

00:15:10.740 --> 00:15:14.010
The toolkit offers a
high level of reusability

00:15:14.010 --> 00:15:17.020
and a very clear
separation of concerns.

00:15:17.020 --> 00:15:19.097
So, if you'll permit,
let's dive in one layer

00:15:19.097 --> 00:15:21.180
deeper and just take a
closer look at the toolkit.

00:15:23.910 --> 00:15:26.290
It's kind of composed
of two levels.

00:15:26.290 --> 00:15:29.130
There's kind of a
back-end data service,

00:15:29.130 --> 00:15:33.750
which is responsible for
proxy-ing API requests

00:15:33.750 --> 00:15:35.160
to Looker.

00:15:35.160 --> 00:15:37.410
And then, on the
front-end SDK side,

00:15:37.410 --> 00:15:43.770
there are some capabilities
to build data visualizations

00:15:43.770 --> 00:15:47.030
in a more kind of recurring way.

00:15:47.030 --> 00:15:50.600
So we'll take a look at
each of those two in turn.

00:15:50.600 --> 00:15:53.360
The architecture
of the application

00:15:53.360 --> 00:15:55.820
is composed of a series
of microservices,

00:15:55.820 --> 00:15:58.680
each really in charge of
their own area of concern.

00:15:58.680 --> 00:16:00.620
So there's one that
does authentication,

00:16:00.620 --> 00:16:04.490
there's another that does
logging, and there is also one,

00:16:04.490 --> 00:16:07.670
of course, that surfaces
ThoughtSpot-backed data

00:16:07.670 --> 00:16:10.670
to power front-end
visualizations.

00:16:10.670 --> 00:16:14.570
We elected to integrate into
this existing architecture

00:16:14.570 --> 00:16:18.920
by building a parallel
Looker-backed data service.

00:16:18.920 --> 00:16:20.720
This had the huge
advantage of not really

00:16:20.720 --> 00:16:23.420
requiring us to rip and replace
any part of the existing

00:16:23.420 --> 00:16:25.190
architecture.

00:16:25.190 --> 00:16:26.690
If we had done so,
it probably would

00:16:26.690 --> 00:16:29.150
have brought product
development to a standstill,

00:16:29.150 --> 00:16:32.880
and probably would have been
met with a lot of resistance,

00:16:32.880 --> 00:16:34.980
let's say--
resistance to change.

00:16:34.980 --> 00:16:37.100
So instead, we just built
this parallel service.

00:16:37.100 --> 00:16:40.520
And without a lot of
fuss, we just started

00:16:40.520 --> 00:16:43.400
doing it in a better, faster
way and let the results

00:16:43.400 --> 00:16:45.800
speak for themselves.

00:16:45.800 --> 00:16:47.630
In a pretty short
amount of time,

00:16:47.630 --> 00:16:49.280
engineering teams
fairly organically

00:16:49.280 --> 00:16:51.410
just started moving
over to using the Looker

00:16:51.410 --> 00:16:55.220
data-backed service because
it just made their jobs easier

00:16:55.220 --> 00:16:59.000
and allowed them to do a
lot more with a lot less.

00:16:59.000 --> 00:17:01.610
The other really good
advantage about this kind

00:17:01.610 --> 00:17:04.940
of data as a sidecar service
is that it exists somewhat

00:17:04.940 --> 00:17:08.060
decoupled from the application
context in which it

00:17:08.060 --> 00:17:09.252
was developed--

00:17:09.252 --> 00:17:11.210
which means it can be
reused in a whole variety

00:17:11.210 --> 00:17:12.900
of different ways.

00:17:12.900 --> 00:17:15.200
We've essentially
built a capability

00:17:15.200 --> 00:17:17.930
as much as we've built
a specific solution.

00:17:17.930 --> 00:17:20.510
And the capability can
be used to, for example,

00:17:20.510 --> 00:17:22.430
power other internal
applications,

00:17:22.430 --> 00:17:26.030
or even externally-facing
applications that our customer

00:17:26.030 --> 00:17:29.420
might want to monetize.

00:17:29.420 --> 00:17:32.300
Given the constraints of
this particular enterprise

00:17:32.300 --> 00:17:36.050
environment, our data service
wasn't built in the Cloud.

00:17:36.050 --> 00:17:39.510
But it's important to note
that, in other circumstances,

00:17:39.510 --> 00:17:43.280
you could use Cloud-based API
type services-- like Apigee,

00:17:43.280 --> 00:17:44.960
for example--

00:17:44.960 --> 00:17:48.870
to play the same role in
this kind of architecture.

00:17:48.870 --> 00:17:52.370
So with that discussion
of the data service,

00:17:52.370 --> 00:17:56.140
let's pivot a little bit and
talk about the development

00:17:56.140 --> 00:17:58.010
toolkit that we
built that handles

00:17:58.010 --> 00:18:01.400
the front-end visualizations.

00:18:01.400 --> 00:18:04.640
For the front-end SDK, we
built a series of reusable

00:18:04.640 --> 00:18:07.850
react components, each one
representing a different data

00:18:07.850 --> 00:18:09.625
visualization type.

00:18:09.625 --> 00:18:11.000
We developed the
interface you're

00:18:11.000 --> 00:18:13.520
looking at here, which exposes
each of the different vis

00:18:13.520 --> 00:18:16.940
components and acts kind
of like live documentation,

00:18:16.940 --> 00:18:19.640
allowing developers,
and product owners,

00:18:19.640 --> 00:18:22.670
and UX designers to
collaborate on the best way

00:18:22.670 --> 00:18:25.110
to tell the given data story.

00:18:25.110 --> 00:18:27.680
Anyone with access can
adjust the vis configuration

00:18:27.680 --> 00:18:29.810
parameters to get
the vis just right

00:18:29.810 --> 00:18:32.330
prior to committing any code.

00:18:32.330 --> 00:18:34.600
We then connected these
components to the Looker data

00:18:34.600 --> 00:18:37.660
service we just spoke about
and parameterized them such

00:18:37.660 --> 00:18:41.980
that we can pass the ID of
any pre-built Looker query

00:18:41.980 --> 00:18:45.850
object connected to the
vis, and essentially render

00:18:45.850 --> 00:18:48.440
the results here in real time.

00:18:48.440 --> 00:18:50.417
Once everybody's happy
with the results,

00:18:50.417 --> 00:18:52.000
it's just a matter,
really, of copying

00:18:52.000 --> 00:18:54.220
a couple of lines
of generated code

00:18:54.220 --> 00:18:58.410
into the React
application code base.

00:18:58.410 --> 00:19:01.230
So by way of both
the Lookup data

00:19:01.230 --> 00:19:04.950
service we spoke about earlier
and this front-end SDK,

00:19:04.950 --> 00:19:08.400
we've been able to leverage
existing capabilities

00:19:08.400 --> 00:19:11.700
within Looker and build a
scalable architecture that

00:19:11.700 --> 00:19:14.880
vastly accelerates
time-to-value for our customers,

00:19:14.880 --> 00:19:17.730
while also decreasing
complexity.

00:19:17.730 --> 00:19:19.410
The net result on
this engagement

00:19:19.410 --> 00:19:21.570
has been a 10x
increase in the speed

00:19:21.570 --> 00:19:24.383
it takes to publish new
insights in the application--

00:19:24.383 --> 00:19:25.800
and all of that
with about a third

00:19:25.800 --> 00:19:30.100
of the engineering
resources required.

00:19:30.100 --> 00:19:34.230
So now, with relevant and timely
insights at their disposal,

00:19:34.230 --> 00:19:36.300
these retail merchants
are using the application

00:19:36.300 --> 00:19:39.030
in much higher numbers and
with much more regularity

00:19:39.030 --> 00:19:40.660
than ever before.

00:19:40.660 --> 00:19:44.430
So from a purely
quantitative perspective,

00:19:44.430 --> 00:19:47.220
I think it's fair to say
achievement unlocked.

00:19:47.220 --> 00:19:51.570
But honestly, the qualitative
outcomes of this engagement,

00:19:51.570 --> 00:19:54.180
to me, are even more exciting.

00:19:54.180 --> 00:19:55.950
What we delivered
initially was a series

00:19:55.950 --> 00:19:59.370
of capabilities offering speed
and flexibility for the product

00:19:59.370 --> 00:20:01.410
team and the engineering team.

00:20:01.410 --> 00:20:04.440
But subsequently, we've
taken these same capabilities

00:20:04.440 --> 00:20:07.260
and offered them to end
users, allowing them, really,

00:20:07.260 --> 00:20:10.180
for the first time, to
self-service insights.

00:20:10.180 --> 00:20:14.290
And as a result, the product has
moved from this very top down,

00:20:14.290 --> 00:20:18.120
one-size-fits-all approach to
analytics to one that really

00:20:18.120 --> 00:20:20.550
promotes much greater
data democratization--

00:20:20.550 --> 00:20:23.610
and ultimately, better
decision-making throughout

00:20:23.610 --> 00:20:24.900
the business.

00:20:24.900 --> 00:20:27.180
The net result is
an organization much

00:20:27.180 --> 00:20:30.330
better prepared to steer the
course through challenging

00:20:30.330 --> 00:20:32.970
economic environments.

00:20:32.970 --> 00:20:34.770
So that pretty much
wraps up what I want

00:20:34.770 --> 00:20:36.210
to share about this engagement.

00:20:36.210 --> 00:20:40.020
I do want to encourage you to
explore how Looker's platform

00:20:40.020 --> 00:20:43.920
capabilities can transform your
own data culture and, in turn,

00:20:43.920 --> 00:20:45.103
your business.

00:20:45.103 --> 00:20:47.520
With that, I'm going to go
ahead and hand it back to Mike.

00:20:50.268 --> 00:20:51.060
MIKE XU: All right.

00:20:51.060 --> 00:20:55.330
Thank you, Nick, for that
walkthrough of the case study.

00:20:55.330 --> 00:20:57.670
So I really hope this
talk gives you guys

00:20:57.670 --> 00:21:01.480
a good sense of the Looker
platform's capabilities,

00:21:01.480 --> 00:21:04.420
how it works and what
it can offer you.

00:21:04.420 --> 00:21:07.240
Hopefully, you're imagining
how it can enable you

00:21:07.240 --> 00:21:10.570
and your team to build
some really cool new data

00:21:10.570 --> 00:21:12.790
experiences.

00:21:12.790 --> 00:21:14.950
Developing with the
right tools can really

00:21:14.950 --> 00:21:17.020
decrease the
complexity, and also

00:21:17.020 --> 00:21:20.310
maintenance cost of
our rich applications.

00:21:20.310 --> 00:21:23.740
And this helps us rapidly
build more and more powerful

00:21:23.740 --> 00:21:24.780
applications as well.

00:21:28.130 --> 00:21:30.940
It's important, really, to
note that Looker is really

00:21:30.940 --> 00:21:35.500
just a piece of the tool set
within Google's development

00:21:35.500 --> 00:21:36.710
environment.

00:21:36.710 --> 00:21:40.120
In Google's ecosystem,
there are lots

00:21:40.120 --> 00:21:42.130
of other tools designed
with developers

00:21:42.130 --> 00:21:44.660
and their applications in mind.

00:21:44.660 --> 00:21:47.700
Together, by using all
of these tools correctly,

00:21:47.700 --> 00:21:49.880
you can really
accelerate development

00:21:49.880 --> 00:21:52.610
and enable your ability
to deliver some custom

00:21:52.610 --> 00:21:54.200
experiences.

00:21:54.200 --> 00:21:58.520
Again, because we're freeing
up resources, or augmenting

00:21:58.520 --> 00:22:02.900
our resources, this new
capacity allows us to really

00:22:02.900 --> 00:22:06.020
innovate and make
groundbreaking business

00:22:06.020 --> 00:22:07.760
discoveries for our end users.

